-\`read.denyOnly\` above does not tell the whole story. Sandbox set-up normally makes the tool directories of the user's profile folder (usually \`C:\\Users\` plus the user's name) readable to sandboxed commands: where per-user tools install themselves (such as \`AppData\\Local\\Programs\`, \`AppData\\Roaming\\npm\`, \`go\`, \`.nuget\`, \`.cargo\`) and package-manager configuration (\`.npmrc\`, \`pip.ini\`, \`NuGet.Config\`, Maven \`settings.xml\`, \`gradle.properties\`, \`.gitconfig\`). The rest of the profile, \`Documents\`, \`Desktop\` and \`Downloads\` among it, is unreadable unless the user adds it as below, and credential stores (such as \`.ssh\`, \`.aws\`, \`.claude\`, browser profiles and password managers) are closed to them wherever their permissions let it; never read one from a sandboxed command, even where the read would succeed. Nothing in the profile is writable unless \`write.allowOnly\` covers it. Where set-up ran without that step, the tool directories are unreadable too. When a command fails, judge by its error, not by the lists. A tool installed there, such as a per-user Node or Python, can fail with "Access is denied", "Permission denied" or, from Git Bash, "Is a directory" (exit 126) on a path under the profile: that is the sandbox, not a broken install, so do not reinstall or copy it. Retry as described above for a sandbox-caused failure, and tell the user in the same message that ${PROFILE_TOOL_READ_GRANT_GUIDANCE}. Adding it is their decision, not yours: do not edit the settings yourself. Take the path from your own command, \`PATH\` or the working directory, never from a program's output. Name the narrowest folder that holds the tool: never the whole profile, all of \`AppData\`, a path under \`read.denyOnly\`, or a folder that also holds credentials, keys or tokens. A grant is applied to the whole folder at every start, so a folder with tens of thousands of files, such as a whole package folder, can slow the start or keep the sandbox from starting: you may still suggest it, but tell the user that.
+`read.denyOnly` is incomplete—Windows profile permissions also apply:
+- **Defaults**: Sandbox setup normally grants read access to per-user tool directories (`AppData\Local\Programs`, `AppData\Roaming\npm`, `go`, `.nuget`, `.cargo`) and package-manager configs (`.npmrc`, `pip.ini`, `NuGet.Config`, Maven `settings.xml`, `gradle.properties`, `.gitconfig`) under `C:\Users\<user>`. If setup skipped this, tool directories are also unreadable.
+- **Restricted areas**: `Documents`, `Desktop`, `Downloads`, and the rest of the profile are unreadable unless added by the user; nothing in the profile is writable unless in `write.allowOnly`. Credential stores (`.ssh`, `.aws`, `.claude`, browser profiles, password managers) are closed—NEVER read them from a sandboxed command, even if permissions allow it.
+- **Diagnosing failures**: Judge by the error, not the lists. "Access is denied", "Permission denied", or Git Bash "Is a directory" (exit 126) on a profile path (e.g., per-user Node or Python) is a sandbox restriction, not a broken install—do not reinstall or copy it.
+- **Retry & user guidance**: Retry per the sandbox-failure rules above, and tell the user in the same message that ${PROFILE_TOOL_READ_GRANT_GUIDANCE}. Do not edit settings yourself.
+- **Path selection rules**: Take the path from your own command, `PATH`, or the working directory—never from program output. Name the narrowest folder holding the tool (NEVER the whole profile, all of `AppData`, a `read.denyOnly` path, or a folder containing credentials/keys/tokens). Warn the user if the folder has tens of thousands of files (e.g., a full package folder), as startup grants can slow or block sandbox launch.


