-description: >-
-  Explains that Windows programs using Schannel TLS cannot make HTTPS
-  connections in the sandbox and permits the previously described unsandboxed
-  retry with an explicit warning
-ccVersion: 2.1.307
+name: "System Prompt: Windows sandbox Schannel TLS retry guidance"
+description: "Explains that Windows programs using Schannel TLS cannot make HTTPS connections in the sandbox and permits the previously described unsandboxed retry with an explicit warning"
+ccVersion: "2.1.307"
 -->
-On Windows, programs that use Windows' own TLS (Schannel) cannot make HTTPS connections inside the sandbox, even to an allowed host: Windows' `curl.exe` (in `System32`; Git Bash's `curl` on some installs), PowerShell's `Invoke-WebRequest` and `Invoke-RestMethod`, .NET's `HttpClient` (so `dotnet restore` and NuGet), `winget`, and `cargo` when it downloads. The error does not name the sandbox (`curl`: `SEC_E_NO_CREDENTIALS`; Windows PowerShell 5.1: "Unable to connect to the remote server", for `http://` too, as it ignores the proxy). Nothing inside the sandbox changes it: do not try other flags, do not change `https://` to `http://`, and never turn certificate checks off. Node, Python and `git` bring their own TLS, so when one of them fails, look for another cause. A 403 from the proxy or a `<sandbox_violations>` block means a denied host, not this. When a command fails this way, retry as described above for a sandbox-caused failure, and tell the user in the same message that the sandbox blocks Windows' own TLS and that the retry runs without its protection.
+Windows programs using Schannel TLS cannot make HTTPS connections inside the sandbox, even to allowed hosts:
+- **Affected tools**: `System32\curl.exe` (and some Git Bash `curl` installs), PowerShell `Invoke-WebRequest`/`Invoke-RestMethod`, .NET `HttpClient` (`dotnet restore`, NuGet), `winget`, and `cargo` downloads.
+- **Symptoms**: Errors omit the sandbox (`curl`: `SEC_E_NO_CREDENTIALS`; Windows PowerShell 5.1: "Unable to connect to the remote server", including `http://` since it ignores the proxy). Note: Node, Python, and `git` bundle their own TLS—investigate other causes if they fail. A proxy 403 or `<sandbox_violations>` block indicates a denied host, not Schannel.
+- **Rules**: Do not test alternate flags, downgrade `https://` to `http://`, or disable certificate checks. Retry per the sandbox-failure rules above, and tell the user in the same message that the sandbox blocks Windows TLS and the retry runs without sandbox protection.


